<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # What's in Your Bag? – Lesson # 5</h2>
<p style="text-align: center;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Discovering Our Classroom</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What's in Your Bag?</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b><br>Mediation: Text: Explain keywords from readings in simpler terms.<br>Communication: Facilitate basic peer interactions in group activities.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b><br>Mediation: Can explain keywords about classroom items with gestures (e.g., points to a "pencil" and says "write with it").<br>Can help peers ask questions about classroom items by pointing or showing objects (e.g., "What's in your bag?" by pointing to a bag).<br>Can clarify meanings by pointing to or holding objects (e.g., holds a "book" and says, "This is for reading.").</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h3 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h3>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher holds up a school bag and asks, "What's in your bag?" The teacher pulls out a "pencil" and asks, "What is this?" and "What do we do with it?" Students respond. The teacher then pulls out a "book" and asks similar questions. This reactivates vocabulary and introduces the idea of explaining functions.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models explaining the function of an item using simple terms and gestures. Holding a "ruler," the teacher says, "This is a ruler. We draw straight lines with it. You measure with it." (mimics drawing a line). The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of /b/ in "book" and /t/ in "table" when handling these objects, and blending for "on" /ɔ/ /-n/.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the explanation concept.
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a ruler for coloring?" (No).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can we draw a straight line with a ruler?" (Yes).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher points to a "pencil case") "What is this for?" (Holding pencils/pens).</li>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">5-7 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Discovering Our Classroom."</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, simple set of illustrated instructions for a mini "Classroom Object Hunt" projected or written on a large chart paper. E.g., "Find the red book. It's on the teacher's desk. Find your pencil. It's in your pencil case." The instructions use familiar classroom vocabulary (desk, book, pencil, pencil case) and prepositions (on, in).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Understanding and explaining simple instructions and clarifying object locations using basic descriptive language and prepositions of place.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads each instruction aloud, pointing to keywords and objects.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What are we looking for?" (Classroom items).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Specific questions:</b> "Where is the red book?" (On the teacher's desk). Students point to the object and location in the classroom.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher models asking, "What's in your bag?" and answers, "My book is in my bag."</li>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">8-10 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Mediation: Explaining object names and functions, and clarifying locations using prepositions. Grammar: WH-questions ("What's in your bag?", "Where is the book?"), Prepositions of place (in, on, next to, between, behind), Present simple (The book is..., You write with it.). Vocabulary: desk, book, marker, board, shelf, eraser, paper, chair, crayon, notebook, ruler, pencil, pencil case, pen, map; find, place, show, hold, look, color, draw, write, erase, read; my, his, her, your.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. "Explain the Word" Charades:</b> The teacher shows a flashcard (e.g., "marker"). Students, individually or chorally, explain its function using simple phrases and gestures: "It's for writing on the board," "You draw with it." (Teacher prompts: "What do you do with a marker?"). Focus on verbs: write, draw, color, erase, read.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. "Where Is It? - Clarify!" Game:</b> The teacher places familiar objects in different locations (e.g., "The eraser is *under* the chair," "The map is *on* the wall."). Students work in pairs. One student asks, "Where is the eraser?" The other student finds it, points, and says, "It's *under* the chair. Here it is!" If needed, the student uses gestures to clarify "under." The teacher reinforces correct pronunciation of prepositions.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Explanation - "My Bag, My Items":</b> Students work in pairs. Each student takes one or two items from their bag or desk (e.g., a "pencil," a "notebook"). Student A shows their item and says, "This is my pencil." Student B asks, "What do you do with it?" Student A explains, "You write with it," using a writing gesture. They swap roles and practice with different items, focusing on clear explanations and respectful interaction (sociolinguistic competence).</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">15-18 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Discovering Our Classroom."</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative "Classroom Scavenger Hunt" with a designated "Mediator" role. This integrates the 21st Century Skills Project.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Divide the class into small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a list for a "Classroom Scavenger Hunt." The list contains 3-4 items to find and describe their location using prepositions (e.g., "Find *a* green crayon.", "Find *the* large map.", "Find *your* notebook.", "Find *a* messy desk.").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Within each group, students take turns being the "Mediator" for one item on the list. The Mediator's role is to:</li>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Read the item aloud to the group.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">If a group member doesn't understand a word (e.g., "crayon," "neat," "behind"), the Mediator must explain it using simpler words, gestures, or by pointing to a similar object. For example, "A crayon? You color with it!" (gestures coloring).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Help the group formulate questions about the item's location (e.g., "Excuse me, where is the map?").</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Once the item is found, the Mediator helps the group confirm its location using prepositions (e.g., "The map is *next to* the board. It's here.").</li>
          </ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups complete the scavenger hunt, rotating the Mediator role with each new item. The teacher circulates, observing and offering support, encouraging respectful classroom interactions.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">12-15 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist during Stage 4. The teacher will observe students specifically in their "Mediator" role.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "Classroom Scavenger Hunt" in groups, focusing on their ability to mediate and facilitate peer understanding and interaction.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Explains keywords (e.g., "pencil" = "write with it") in simpler terms using gestures.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Helps peers ask or answer questions about classroom items (e.g., prompts "What's in your bag?").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarifies meanings by pointing to or holding objects (e.g., holds a "book" and says, "This is for reading.").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (nouns, verbs, adjectives, prepositions) intelligibly.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Facilitates peer interaction respectfully (e.g., "Excuse me...").</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket:</p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I help my friends understand new words or find things today?" (Circle: Yes / A little / No)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I explain what a 'book' is for?" (Circle: Yes / No)</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">8-10 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompts:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy about helping my friends understand words or find things during our scavenger hunt?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was difficult about explaining things to my friends today, and what could I do better next time?"</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">"Did students successfully explain vocabulary and facilitate peer interaction during the scavenger hunt? Were they able to use gestures, pointing, and simpler terms effectively to clarify meanings? How well did they integrate the new vocabulary and grammatical structures (prepositions, WH-questions) in their explanations? What adjustments are needed for supporting mediation skills in future lessons, especially for different learning styles or specific vocabulary items?"</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "What's in Your Bag?" theme and the 5-lesson sequence by integrating all learned skills and vocabulary through the 21st Century Project. The learning from this theme, particularly the ability to identify and describe classroom objects and their locations, will serve as a foundational building block for future themes involving descriptions of environments and daily routines.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">5 min</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>